  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1130 AM CST Sun Nov 8 2026\n\nDry cold front has largely shifted winds to the west/northwest as\nit continues to track over Lake Michigan early this afternoon. \nAs such, surface obs show widespread gusts between 20 and 25 mph,\nhighest along the lakeshore in proximity to the front. Wind shift\nhas likewise resulted in lake-induced stratus deck sinking south \ninto portions of north-central Wisconsin, which should stick \naround through the overnight period.\n\nAbove normal temperatures in the mid to upper 50s persist through\nTuesday as warming ridge builds over the Plains. Next frontal \npassage (more adjacent to a wind shift) then remains on track for\nlate Monday into Tuesday. Cold FROPA looks to move through mostly\ndry during this time, with ensembles generating light QPF \nWednesday into Thursday as upper trough digs down from Manitoba \ninto the Great Lakes. Blend retains 20 to 30% PoPs, mainly \nconcentrated in the lake effect belts. Northerly flow regime will \nresult in cold air core working south into the Great Lakes by the \nmiddle to end of the week, with 850 mb temperatures plunging to \nnear -10C. Therefore, potential exists for a few flurries to mix \nin with light rain at times. Steady stream of CAA paired with \nclosed upper low sitting and spinning over the eastern CONUS may \nbe enough to keep the flurry machine going in Vilas co and along \nthe lakeshore through the end of the work week.\n\nOutlook... Solid signal (50 to 70%) exists for above normal\ntemperatures through at least the end of the month with El Nino \ndriving the synoptic pattern. Additionally, omega ridge looks to\nset up toward the end of the week, which should result in a\nprolonged period of dry weather to accompany the mild conditions.\nWith D4 drought in far northern Wisconsin, will have to monitor\nfor fire weather concerns over the next several weeks.",
